well just over two miles from here more  than 100 workers at the mayfield  consumer products factory were making  candles when a tornado  tore through the building at least eight  of them were killed workers say they  were told to huddle in a hallway which  was the strongest part of the building  as the storm approached many say they  saw the roof peel away and the walls  cave in  the company says eight other workers are  still missing our lead national  correspondent david begno is following  that story and is here with me and david  just to think that many of these workers  were there doing overtime working around  the clock to get these candles ready for  the holidays you know i interviewed the  ceo of the company and he said we have  eight people dead i said wait a minute  the governor said 80 to 100. how do you  only have eight there's a big  discrepancy here and he said well we  thought that many were dead because we  couldn't reach him  couldn't make a call and he said we  ended up over the next 24 36 hours  tracking them down and that's why the  apparent death toll is so much smaller  than what we were led to believe it  could be  you know just about as soon as we got  here within the first two to three hours  we started talking to survivors  who were tearfully telling us what it  was like to survive the tornado  and to then get out you're going to hear  from four of them now  they told us it was a tornado warning  coming and everybody needed to go for  shelters and all of a sudden everything  started caving in on everybody nobody  seen it coming this is barbara tate  she worked at the mayfield consumer  products factory for two years right  alongside her brother who was there when  the tornado hit and also got out alive  yes it was a lot of stuff to fail on us  but we we had a crowd  crowd like like like you know get on  your knees and everything crawl and try  because we couldn't move a certain way  or everything would fall on top of us  this is another survivor richard padilla  he joined other workers in a central  hallway the strongest part of the  building when the alarms sounded do you  need some help  he is still in so much pain after what  he experienced the ceiling  blows up  and a couple of seconds  after that  a wall  fall  above us  and then  we were in a position  very uncomfortable  with a lot of things over us  and then i finally saw somebody  who  was giving his hand to me  meet felicia lynn she had only been  working at the factory since september  and was also in that central hallway  when the roof was ripped off and the  walls collapsed my both leg was twisted  my arm is twisted to release my feet i  have to let go my two shoes there was a  small light in there so i climb up  myself  she limped out of the wreckage and  recorded this video you hear her  repeatedly saying thank you god for  saving me  but thank you for learning  she also credits this man nelson  gonzalez her supervisor for rescuing her  he was the one who helped to free her  legs so that she could get out i was  making sure that the people was next to  me  i wasn't going to leave them behind  i'm not going to let them down  they're my families as well  nelson and richard had recently moved  here from puerto rico they were looking  for work and the candle factory needed  seasonal workers  they were working the night shift along  with barbara and felicia  four of the 110 people inside that  factory when it got hit i closed my eyes  and all i see is people hollering and  screaming for him so i'm not doing good  at all  you're messing with a person when you  see that and you can't help anybody  you try to help yourself get out  you understand why miss barbara feels a  sense of guilt for not being able to  help more people listen nelson is headed  back to puerto rico today with his son  who worked the day shift and  is okay he wasn't there nelson said he's  not sure if he's coming back nor his  wife told him you could tell everybody  goodbye because i'm not letting you  go back the owner of the factory has  started a account a gofundme to raise  money and the owner of the factory has  given a hundred thousand dollars to get  a kick started to help the people what  so many people have gone through here  david bagno thank you
 

